   178	| K v7.1.293 backends and builtin integer theory | Executes and proves every K claim | Version checks, successful compilation, `#Top`, and discriminating negative probes |
   179	| Supplied `py2mpy.py` | Translates `solution.py` to `solution.mpy` | Regeneration exits 0; `check-program-identity.py` confirms the source AST and the body shared with the claim |
   180	| MPY-to-intended-Python correspondence for the exercised subset | Connects the theorem under the supplied semantics to the HumanEval contract | The fixed `pyMod` definition uses floored remainder; all four prompt examples and two extra negative cases execute successfully |
   181	
   182	No program-derived value is opaque, no execution is bypassed, and no result-
   183	bearing external primitive is used.
   184	
   185	# Empirical evidence
   186	
   187	`concrete-tests.py` contains the four prompt examples:
   188	
   189	```text
   190	(148, 412) -> 16
   191	(19, 28) -> 72
   192	(2020, 1851) -> 0
   193	(14, -15) -> 20
   194	```
   195	
   196	It also checks `(-14, 15) -> 30` and `(-14, -15) -> 30`, documenting Python's
   197	nonnegative remainder for a positive divisor. The LLVM run completed with no
   198	assertion exception. These six ground cases support intent validation; the
   199	universal result comes from `SPEC.multiply-correct`, not from testing.
   200	
   201	# Excluded behavior
   202	
   203	- Non-integer arguments are excluded by the prompt's valid-input contract.
   204	- Behavior under a Python implementation other than the supplied MPY model is
   205	  conditional on the model-correspondence trust boundary above.
   206	- No separate total-correctness or resource-bound theorem is claimed.

